          "speaker": "Mia",
          "text": "Like what?"
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-22",
          "speaker": "Rob",
          "text": "Well, she found these articles from the 1950s about how relationships between children and their parents, or between the public and people like bank managers or the police were shifting."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-23",
          "speaker": "Mia",
          "text": "Interesting. And did you have any practical problems to overcome?"
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-24",
          "speaker": "Rob",
          "text": "Well, in the final rehearsal everything was going fine until the last scene - what’s where the doctor’s first patient appears on stage on his own."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-25",
          "speaker": "Mia",
          "text": "The one in the wheelchair?"
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-26",
          "speaker": "Rob",
          "text": "Yes, and he had this really long speech, with the stage all dark except for one spotlight - and then that stuck somehow so it was shining on the wrong side of the stage … but anyway we got that fixed, thank goodness."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-27",
          "speaker": "Mia",
          "text": "Yes, it was fine on the night."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-28",
          "speaker": "Rob",
          "text": "But while you’re here, Mia. I wanted to ask you about the year abroad option. Would you recommend doing that?"
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-29",
          "speaker": "Mia",
          "text": "Yes, definitely. It’s a fantastic chance to study in another country for a year."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-30",
          "speaker": "Rob",
          "text": "I think I’d like to do it, but it looks very competitive - there’s only a limited number of places."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-31",
          "speaker": "Mia",
          "text": "Yes, so next year when you are in the second year of the course, you need to work really hard in all your theatre studies modules. Only students with good marks get places - you have to prove that you know your subject really well."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-32",
          "speaker": "Rob",
          "text": "Right. So how did you choose where to go?"
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-33",
          "speaker": "Mia",
          "text": "Well, I decided I wanted a programme that would fit in with what I wanted to do after I graduate, so I looked for a university with emphasis on acting rather than directing for example. It depends on you. Then about six months before you go, you have to email the scheme coordinator with your top three choices. I had a friend who missed the deadline and didn’t get her first choice, so you do need to get a move on at that stage. You’ll find that certain places are very popular with everyone."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-34",
          "speaker": "Rob",
          "text": "And don’t you have to write a personal statement at that stage?"
        },
